of the data center space.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.180
We're big believers in the growth of data.

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:59.370
We own Cannella which is a leading

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.455
direct response television
advertising company.

00:10:01.455 --> 00:10:04.730
AdThrive, a digital advertising company,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:06.298
and a number of other interests.

00:10:06.298 --> 00:10:09.268
- And what's happening with
your day-to-day these days

00:10:09.268 --> 00:10:11.090
within that ecosystem?

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.527
- My day-to-day is I'm a partner at ZMC

00:10:13.527 --> 00:10:15.499
and I'm the chief executive of Take-Two.

00:10:15.499 --> 00:10:20.499
But Take-Two is a part of
ZMC, so I have the luxury

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.144
that I'm able to both invest and operate

00:10:23.144 --> 00:10:26.071
in the media business, and I am able to,

00:10:26.071 --> 00:10:30.360
our entire thesis is
based around the notion

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:33.040
that technology will continue to transform

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.225
and inform media and entertainment.

00:10:35.225 --> 00:10:37.410
And we only invest behind that thesis.

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:38.700
And the reason that we've never suffered

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.370
a loss, and we've been able to grow

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.041
a very significant business from nothing,

00:10:42.041 --> 00:10:44.845
at least so far, is because we don't

00:10:44.845 --> 00:10:48.125
look at what's happening tomorrow

00:10:48.125 --> 00:10:51.970
or next quarter, or a year, we look

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:53.870
five, 10, 15 20 years down the road.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:55.360
And so far we haven't gotten it wrong.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.880
So in '01, people in our business,

00:10:57.880 --> 00:10:59.190
and we did have access to capital

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:00.900
or we couldn't have
bought these companies.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.280
People in our business
were buying newspapers,

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:05.700
consumer magazines, and that was sexy

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.760
and appealing and we didn't do those deals

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.320
because we thought print was compromised.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.570
And of course we were
right, we did however,

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:13.150
by an online market research company

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:15.180
before that was sexy, and obviously took

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:16.849
over a video game company in '07

00:11:16.849 --> 00:11:19.160
that was very nearly bankrupt.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.747
And the perception was that company

00:11:20.747 --> 00:11:22.690
was at no chance of making it.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:27.639
That companies market cap
today is about $10 billion.

00:11:27.639 --> 00:11:32.639
- What's kind of most
exciting you right now

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:35.523
as you look out five, 10, 15 years?

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:39.750
- I think, this is just the beginning.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.217
So for a lot of people, a
lot of people think that,

00:11:41.217 --> 00:11:42.991
and I just heard this at a conference.

00:11:42.991 --> 00:11:45.070
You know, we've kind of established

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:46.570
what the internet can do, we've kind of

00:11:46.570 --> 00:11:48.610
established what digital
technology can do,

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:49.912
it's all gonna settle down,
and now the move forward

00:11:49.912 --> 00:11:54.210
in digitally driven media
will be evolutionary

00:11:54.210 --> 00:11:56.580
not revolutionary, I completely disagree.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:58.400
I think the next 30 or 40 years

00:11:58.400 --> 00:11:59.581
will be the most exciting time

00:11:59.581 --> 00:12:02.510
that the media business has ever seen.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:04.090
And you will see it in every part,

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:06.030
whether it's B2B, which we're in

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:09.590
or B2C, which we're in,
or pure entertainment,

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:13.020
which we're in, or
infrastructure, which we're in.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:14.830
And the reason I believe in that,

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.523
is several things --

00:12:16.523 --> 00:12:17.570
- History.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.145
- Well first is history right --

00:12:19.145 --> 00:12:20.053
- But honestly, right?

00:12:20.922 --> 00:12:22.200
I mean that's the answer.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:23.447
- Yeah I mean in the 1880's you know,

00:12:23.447 --> 00:12:24.770
the head of the U.S. Patent Office

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:26.538
said I think pretty much
everything has been invented.

00:12:26.538 --> 00:12:27.371
- That's it.

00:12:27.371 --> 00:12:28.813
- So you know --

00:12:28.813 --> 00:12:29.646
- You just know what humans do.

00:12:29.646 --> 00:12:30.760
- Yeah we just keep inventing.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.740
- Either their framework or perspective

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:35.320
doesn't allow them to historically
understand the context,

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:38.680
or they are financially
invested in the outcome

00:12:38.680 --> 00:12:39.550
that comes out of their mouth.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:41.400
- And often, frankly,
emotionally invested.

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:42.370
- 100%.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:43.740
- Because everyone's financially invested

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.430
in staying ahead of change.

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:46.570
It's more emotionally invested.

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.105
Change scares a lot of people.

00:12:48.105 --> 00:12:48.938
- But I think you'll
appreciate this right,

00:12:48.938 --> 00:12:51.170
like to me, I'm of course
financially invested

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:52.902
in all of the things I do, however

00:12:52.902 --> 00:12:55.500
because I'm an entrepreneur, I'm putting

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:57.940
my money where my mouth is, versus hoping

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.790
that the things maintains for the next

00:13:00.790 --> 00:13:04.000
27 months because I need to
sell my stock at 27 months

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.500
and go do what I wanna
do personally after that.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:07.830
- Yeah, that's a horrible way to live.

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.490
By the way, whenever people give me a

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.150
story like that, I'm like,
well here's the problem

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:12.480
with that plan, you could walk outside

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:13.550
and get hit by a truck tomorrow.

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:15.650
You can't even be careful
about delaying things for that.

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:17.750
So anyway, I think you
know, if you believe

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.170
in Moore's Law, which
I do, you believe that

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:22.711
whatever data consumption
you wanna project

00:13:22.711 --> 00:13:25.520
five years from now,
aggregate data consumption,

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:26.900
you're wrong, it will be more.

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:27.920
- Couldn't agree more.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.270
- You wanna project what
devices will look like.

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:32.614
So Nick's holding an iPhone,
I don't know which one.

00:13:32.614 --> 00:13:34.280
Looks like a nine.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:36.180
- If it's mine it's new.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.551
- All right, or a 10.

00:13:37.551 --> 00:13:40.700
So that form factor, in 10 years,

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:42.040
we'll look back on that form factor

00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:43.550
and we will think it's really funny.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:45.470
Looking at a couple cameras in here,

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.600
these form factors will look
really funny in 10 years.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:48.840
- I just don't understand
that people don't understand.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:49.673
It's history.

00:13:49.673 --> 00:13:50.745
- Right, so --

00:13:50.745 --> 00:13:52.450
- You know how rad as
fuck the Walkman was?

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:53.283
I know you do.

00:13:53.283 --> 00:13:54.116
- Well yeah, of course.

00:13:54.116 --> 00:13:54.949
- And like it was just the crazy --

00:13:54.949 --> 00:13:56.170
- You just couldn't believe it.

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:57.810
- The Razor phone was the single

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:00.720
most important status item,
Seth you're old enough?

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:03.276
I'm impressed, like yeah, people are

00:14:03.276 --> 00:14:07.600
unbelievably confused about
how it actually works.

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:10.470
- So what we do, is we look at the world

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:11.960
and we try to think about where

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.550
that's gonna be in five or 10 years.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:16.600
And then, to the point about preservation

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:20.720
versus innovation or disintermediation,

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:23.710
we gotta be the ones to
disintermediate ourselves.

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:24.543
- 100%

00:14:24.543 --> 00:14:26.020
- And that's a very hard task.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:27.810
But it you're worried
about cannibalization

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.810
in your core business, you should be

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:30.643
the one cannibalizing your core business.

00:14:30.643 --> 00:14:31.476
Or someone else will do it for you.

00:14:31.476 --> 00:14:34.100
- Something I said to
a buddy a long time ago

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:35.840
that I use a lot, it was
the first time I said it

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:36.673
and I'll never forget I said it,

00:14:36.673 --> 00:14:38.590
I said look I'd rather
put myself out of business

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:40.250
then let somebody else do it for me.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:41.083
- [Strauss] Exactly.

00:14:41.083 --> 00:14:41.970
- It's exactly right.

00:14:41.970 --> 00:14:44.960
- When I got to the
record business in '94,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.510
the first things we
did was launch websites

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:49.900
which was innovative,
and start thinking about

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.440
how we can do digital distribution.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:52.980
By the way we didn't do such a great job

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.280
but we were trying really hard.

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:57.300
And we weren't surprised
when the Napsters came along.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.347
We were prepared for it and we were

00:14:59.347 --> 00:15:00.254
in the best position from anyone.

00:15:00.254 --> 00:15:01.813
- What do you think about blockchain?

00:15:01.813 --> 00:15:02.976
Forget about bitcoin and all
of that stuff, blockchain.

00:15:02.976 --> 00:15:06.610
- Look, I think the notion
of a widely distributed

00:15:06.610 --> 00:15:09.630
network for computing is fine, the problem

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:11.150
with the blockchain technology is

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:14.270
so far anyway, for you to create

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:15.520
the additional nodes you have to create

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:17.470
an incentive for people
to create the nodes.

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:19.241
And so far the incentive
has been currency.

00:15:19.241 --> 00:15:20.074
- [Gary] That's right.

00:15:20.074 --> 00:15:21.603
- And so if you have
something on the one hand

00:15:21.603 --> 00:15:23.810
that has no owner and
is highly distributed,

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:25.170
on the other hand has no

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.970
built-in incentive, bit of a question.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:28.530
The internet had a built in incentive.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:30.076
Which is, I wanna express myself.

00:15:30.076 --> 00:15:32.837
Or I wanna transact, or I wanna

00:15:32.837 --> 00:15:34.270
have you come see something.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:35.430
- It was human.

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.490
- But it's hard to know in the absence

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:41.020
of currency how you're gonna
make the blockchain work.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:42.530
And then when companies talk about it

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.280
I'm convinced, when
big, big, big companies

00:15:45.280 --> 00:15:46.113
are, I mean, people talk to us about

00:15:46.113 --> 00:15:47.870
it in the game space all the time.

00:15:47.870 --> 00:15:48.703
I wanna apply blockchain technology

00:15:48.703 --> 00:15:50.570
to the video game business, I'm like,

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:53.258
please tell me how that
works, and it's not sarcastic.

00:15:53.258 --> 00:15:54.091
I'm very interested.

00:15:54.091 --> 00:15:55.840
- You just know that
they're putting hyperbole

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:57.277
around the statement,
I agree with you 100%.

00:15:57.277 --> 00:16:00.160
- And so right now, like do
I believe in crypto currency?

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.320
I don't, and I know there
are plenty of people that do.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:05.050
My attitude is, I believe in speculations.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:07.100
They exist all the time, but any time

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:09.019
a so-called currency goes from $10

00:16:09.019 --> 00:16:13.230
to $20,000 to $3,600 in the space of like,

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:14.237
my recent memory,

00:16:14.237 --> 00:16:15.070
(Gary laughs)

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:16.710
that's not a currency,
it's something else,

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:19.880
it's not a currency, and for people

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:22.010
who transact outside a legitimate economy

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:23.350
they don't really care, because their

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:25.638
margins are so huge that
what they care about

00:16:25.638 --> 00:16:29.176
is private transactions,
but for the rest of us,

00:16:29.176 --> 00:16:31.240
like I have a really high margin

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.020
in most of my businesses, but I don't

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:34.820
have a sufficient margin to offset

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:35.850
that kind of currency swing.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:36.683
- I understand.

00:16:36.683 --> 00:16:39.195
- So I'm not a believer
at all in crypto currency.

00:16:39.195 --> 00:16:41.029
- What about sports betting?

00:16:41.029 --> 00:16:42.680
- I'm a huge believer in sports,

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:44.150
well first of all, it's a little late

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:46.247
in the day since the U.S. Government said

00:16:46.247 --> 00:16:47.220
"we believe in it".

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.940
We're taking it from
the Federal government

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:52.357
saying it's okay, to
the states regulating it

00:16:52.357 --> 00:16:53.970
in a way that works, will be complicated.

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.330
And then you've got a very powerful --

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:56.610
- I grew up in the liquor business,

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.740
so between cannabis and sports betting,

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:00.927
two things in the macro
I deeply believe in.

00:17:00.927 --> 00:17:01.870
- Right but micro's different.

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:04.340
And so you've got massive lobbies,

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:07.000
very powerful lobbies, who've proven,

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:07.999
by the way that they can affect

00:17:07.999 --> 00:17:10.980
a legislation of late, who have a --

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:12.790
- Not just of late, I'm they're ...

00:17:12.790 --> 00:17:14.143
- Right, but in the casino business where,

00:17:14.143 --> 00:17:15.990
so what do I believe, that if you're

00:17:15.990 --> 00:17:17.930
already in the casino
business in the U.S.,

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.149
will you be able to engage
in broadly distributed,

00:17:20.149 --> 00:17:22.730
digitally driven sports betting?

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:27.500
Yes, do I believe that
we as a non-regulated,

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:30.542
non-gaming company can
take our sports titles

00:17:30.542 --> 00:17:33.677
and turn them into gaming opportunities?

00:17:33.677 --> 00:17:35.360
It would be wonderful to believe that,

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:37.820
and if I were in the business of promoting

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:40.100
our security publicly I would say yes.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:41.340
I think the answer is that the opportunity

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:43.523
may exist, but there are a
lot of regulatory hurdles.

00:17:43.523 --> 00:17:46.993
There are obviously far fewer
of them internationally.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:50.912
- Talk to me about,
actually I'm gonna ask you

00:17:50.912 --> 00:17:51.970
a question that I'm curious about.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:54.630
So I've been really enjoying,
similar to blockchain

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:56.824
and cannabis, and sports betting,

00:17:56.824 --> 00:18:00.924
the notion of eSports teams,
watching a lot of people

00:18:00.924 --> 00:18:04.790
deploy the logic around something,

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.170
you know this is where I get interested.

00:18:06.170 --> 00:18:09.163
I think this is from afar,
and you know it's very clear

00:18:09.163 --> 00:18:11.223
to me, listening to you,
there's a lot of things

00:18:11.223 --> 00:18:13.460
that we cross over in agreeing on.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.330
I'm fascinated by people's inability

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:17.882
to synthesize things in
lieu of what they hope

00:18:17.882 --> 00:18:21.410
or emotionally or
financially want to happen.

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:23.730
Watching people deploy eSports strategies

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:25.640
in today's environments, fascinating

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.370
because much like what you did to whoever

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:29.027
says to you, we're gonna
do this blockchain thing,

00:18:29.027 --> 00:18:30.840
I always say the same thing, I'm like,

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:32.440
please explain to me why you think

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:35.460
League of Legends is like the
Major League Baseball system.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:36.950
And when you get into even a basic

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.573
first question, and you watch
people dance in front of you,

00:18:42.356 --> 00:18:44.060
now that I've got a better framework

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.058
of how you process, and it's probably why

00:18:46.058 --> 00:18:48.470
I like you, you know, what's your take

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.300
on the eSports ecosystem
around the IP holders

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:54.310
versus how the teams think they're

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:55.400
gonna conglomerate the power?

00:18:55.400 --> 00:18:56.700
- So we're in the business, right?

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.680
And we eat our own cooking, but our view

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:01.067
is that like major sports,
we believe in eSports.

00:19:01.067 --> 00:19:03.984
There are 250 million
people who watch eSports.

00:19:03.984 --> 00:19:04.880
There are a whole bunch
of people who are playing.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:05.713
- You're preaching.

00:19:05.713 --> 00:19:07.346
- So for sure it's real, it is however,

00:19:07.346 --> 00:19:09.205
the League of Legends business,

00:19:09.205 --> 00:19:10.810
and it's a billion dollar business.

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:12.830
And in our world, video
game business alone,

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:15.575
never mind the media
business, video game business

00:19:15.575 --> 00:19:17.850
is a $130 billion a year currently.

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:20.540
Esports is a billion dollars,
it's a teeny little business

00:19:20.540 --> 00:19:22.140
and the bulk of the market
is League of Legends.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:24.771
And I say that as a competitor,
we have the NBA2K league.

00:19:24.771 --> 00:19:29.140
So I think they'll be somewhere between

00:19:29.140 --> 00:19:31.590
three and seven powerful eSports

00:19:31.590 --> 00:19:33.220
that will develop in the same way

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:34.860
that there are between three and seven

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:37.020
professional sports
leagues that truly matter.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.130
You know, you've got
football, you've got baseball,

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:41.240
you've got soccer, especially worldwide.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.580
- So you believe some of these IPs

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.011
will come out, really lock in --

